{%- comment -%}
  PG Wall Art gallery.

  The same two changes pg-gallery-tweaks makes on the crystal orbs page,
  applied to the wall art page:

  1. The room shot that used to stand on top of the gallery was dropped at the
     owner's request. Hiding .pgx-hero is what lifts the lenticular flip video
     into that slot, because the video is the next child in pg-landing's DOM.
  2. Below it, pg-landing lays the photo strip out two-up on desktop. That
     sizing was for thumbnails sitting UNDER a big hero; with the hero gone the
     strip IS the gallery, so a pair of small tiles is too little to be the only
     picture of the product. Desktop now shows one photo per view, paged by the
     arrows pg-landing already draws at that breakpoint.

  Under 900px the strip is already one photo per view: pg-gallery-mobile does
  that site-wide from footer-group. This file only has to handle desktop.

  The hero is HIDDEN, not deleted, and no product media was touched. That photo
  is still the product's featured image, so it goes on carrying the collection
  card, the bundle tile and the cart line. The strip's buttons still set the
  hidden hero's src; they simply set it on something nobody sees.

  3. The single gallery video is now a ten-slide video slideshow, one clip per
     variant, and selecting a design anywhere on the page -- the buy-box
     dropdowns or the variant picker inside the cart drawer -- moves the
     slideshow to that design's clip. A swipe and the dots page through it by
     hand; there are no arrow buttons, at any width -- see note 6.
     The nine newer clips play at 2.5x, the original at normal speed; the rate
     is per clip, as `r` in the script.

  WHY THE SLIDESHOW IS BUILT IN JS RATHER THAN IN pg-landing. That section is
  shared by the homepage and five product templates and renders ONE video from
  its `gallery_video_url` setting. Teaching it about a per-variant video list
  would push wall-art-only markup into every page that uses it. This file is
  scoped to this template, so the whole feature lives here and pg-landing is
  untouched. If the JS never runs, pg-landing's original single <video> is what
  stays on screen -- the takeover is the enhancement, not the baseline.

  TWO VIDEO ELEMENTS, NOT TEN. Ten <video> tags would have the browser opening
  ten connections for clips nobody asked for. Swapping `src` on ONE element
  blanks it to black the instant the new source is set, which is a visible flash
  on every variant change. So there are two stacked elements and the incoming
  clip is loaded off-screen, faded in only once it has frames to show, and the
  outgoing one is paused after the fade. Nothing but the first clip is fetched
  until a shopper actually asks for it.

  ASPECT. Every slide is the same size, 990x1080 -- the shape of the original
  gallery clip, which is what set the height of this slot before there was a
  slideshow. The box never changes, so paging or switching variant never moves
  the page.

  The nine newer clips do not natively fit that box. They are 1920x1080 FILES
  with roughly 9:16 footage pillarboxed inside them; the black bars are baked
  into the frames, not letterboxing added by the layout. Three attempts, and
  only the third holds:

    - CONTAIN in a 16:9 box. Reads the files as genuine 16:9 content and fits
      the whole frame in, so every baked-in bar stays on screen and the footage
      shrinks to make room for them.
    - COVER in the 990x1080 box. Cover only crops what OVERFLOWS. Tall narrow
      footage scaled into a wider box does not overflow it sideways, it falls
      SHORT -- measured on the page, the footage reached about 64% of the box
      width and the remaining 36% stayed black. Cover cannot remove a bar the
      box is too wide to hide.
    - ZOOM, which is this. `z` on each entry scales the element up until the
      footage itself is wider than the box, so the bars are pushed off both
      edges and clipped away.

  WHERE 1.66 COMES FROM. Cover already fits the file's height to the box, so the
  footage lands at (footage aspect / box aspect) of the box width. For 9:16 in
  990x1080 that is 0.5625 / 0.9167 = 0.61, and the page measured about 0.64;
  1 / 0.61 = 1.63 is the theoretical fill and 1 / 0.64 = 1.56 the measured one.
  1.66 clears both with a few percent to spare, because a bar left showing is
  the visible failure here and a few extra cropped pixels are not.

  WHAT IT COSTS. Zooming past the width crops the height: about 40% of these
  clips, a fifth off the top and a fifth off the bottom. That is inherent -- 9:16
  footage cannot fill a 0.9167 box any other way. The clips are framed with the
  poster centred, so the crop takes room rather than subject, but it IS a crop.
  Re-encoding the nine to 990x1080 with the footage centred would let `z` go
  back to 1 and cost nothing at all.

  4. THE DESIGN NAME, ON EVERY VIDEO AND EVERY PHOTO. Same pill the orb page
     carries (pg-gallery-tweaks), for the same reason: with ten designs sharing
     one product page, nothing on screen said which one you were looking at.

     The video's name is free -- the slideshow already knows which variant it is
     showing, so the pill is written from VIDS[i].v at the swap.

     The PHOTOS are the harder half, because the strip's own markup carries no
     design: pg-landing renders every thumbnail with alt "Holo Legends Wall Art
     view N". The names come from the product's MEDIA alt text instead, shipped
     inline as JSON so the first paint has them rather than waiting on a
     round trip.

     MATCHED ON FILENAME, THEN ON KEYWORD. Each button's own <img> src filename
     is matched against the media list -- never button i to media[i+1], which
     works until the next upload and then labels every photo with its
     neighbour's. The matched alt is then read for a character name and mapped
     to the variant that sells it ("Gastly" -> Gengar Evolution). Order inside
     DESIGNS matters: the EX rule has to be tested before the plain Blastoise
     one, or "Blastoise EX" lands on Blastoise Evolution. A photo that matches
     nothing gets NO label rather than a wrong one.

  5. THE SWIPE HINT on the photo strip, also from the orb page.
     pg-gallery-mobile hides the strip's arrows under 900px because they sat on
     top of the artwork, which left no sign the strip scrolls at all -- 28 photos reading as a dead row. The pill
     sits ON the strip, pinned to the top edge of the photo, so it is
     unambiguously about the photos rather than about the video above them, and
     it carries the position (4/28) so it answers "how many more" and not just
     "there are more". Phones only: desktop keeps the real arrows.

     It is a standing label, never dismissed. The orb page tried removing it on
     first scroll and then on first touchmove, and both were too eager -- a
     finger that starts on the gallery and drags DOWN scrolls the page, which
     fires touchmove on the strip, so simply scrolling past the photos killed
     the hint.

  6. THE VIDEO SLIDESHOW IS SWIPED, NOT CLICKED. The two purple chevrons that
     used to sit on the artwork were dropped at the owner's request, replaced by
     the same pill the photo strip carries.

     THE HINT AND THE GESTURE SHIP TOGETHER. The photo strip is a real
     overflow-x scroller, so its pill only had to LABEL a gesture the browser
     already handled. The video stage is not a scroller -- it is two stacked,
     absolutely positioned <video> elements -- so nothing would have answered a
     finger there. Removing the arrows without adding the gesture would leave a
     pill advertising a swipe that does nothing, so the drag handler below is
     the substantive half of this change and the pill is the label on it.

     Pointer events, so one path covers touch, pen and a desktop mouse drag --
     the arrows were the only control at desktop widths too, and the dots alone
     are a poor replacement for ten slides. The gesture commits to horizontal
     only once it has moved further sideways than down (10px), and only then
     starts calling preventDefault, so a finger that lands on the video and
     drags DOWN still scrolls the page. `touch-action:pan-y` on the stage says
     the same thing to the compositor, which is what keeps the page scrolling at
     60fps instead of waiting on this handler. A gesture that starts on a dot is
     ignored so the dots stay clickable.

     The counter is painted from paintDots(), which already runs on every slide
     change from every source -- arrow, dot, swipe, or a variant being chosen
     elsewhere on the page -- so it cannot drift out of step with the slide.

  Rendered only from templates/product.pg-wallart.json, so removing it from
  that template's order puts the original gallery back in one edit.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  The media list, rendered rather than fetched, so the name is on the first
  paint. The fetch fallback the orb page keeps is not repeated here: this
  section only ever renders on the wall art product template, where `product` is
  always in scope.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if product and product.media.size > 0 -%}
<script type="application/json" id="pgw-media">
[{%- for m in product.media -%}{%- unless forloop.first -%},{%- endunless -%}{"a":{{ m.alt | default: '' | json }},"s":{{ m.preview_image.src | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last | json }}}{%- endfor -%}]
</script>
{%- endif -%}
<style>
  /* the room shot -- see the note above */
  #pgx .pgx-hero{display:none !important}
  /* the video leads the column now, so it does not need the gap that used to
     separate it from the hero */
  #pgx .pgx-vid{margin-top:0 !important}

  /* the strip is the gallery now, not a row of thumbnails under one */
  @media (min-width:901px){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgx-thumbs button{flex:0 0 100% !important}
  }

  /* ---- the video slideshow ---- */
  /* one box for all ten slides: the original clip's shape. overflow:hidden is
     load-bearing, not tidiness -- it is what clips the zoomed-off bars. */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-stage{position:relative;width:100%;aspect-ratio:990/1080;background:#0d0d0d;overflow:hidden}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-v{
    position:absolute !important;inset:0 !important;
    width:100% !important;height:100% !important;
    object-fit:cover !important;display:block !important;
    transform-origin:center center !important;   /* zoom out of the middle, where the footage is */
    opacity:0;transition:opacity .28s ease;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-v.on{opacity:1}

  /* THE ARROWS ARE GONE -- see note 6. The stage is dragged instead, so it has
     to tell the compositor which axis it wants: pan-y hands vertical scrolling
     straight to the browser (the page keeps scrolling through the video at full
     speed) and reserves horizontal for the handler in the script. Without it a
     horizontal drag is claimed as a page pan before preventDefault can run.
     Selection is off so a slow drag does not start highlighting the page, and
     the grab cursor is the desktop affordance the chevrons used to be. */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-stage{
    touch-action:pan-y;
    -webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;
    cursor:grab;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-stage.pgw-drag{cursor:grabbing}

  /* the swipe hint, pinned to the top of the photo the strip is showing.
     .pgx-thumbwrap is position:relative on both breakpoints (pg-landing sets it,
     pg-gallery-mobile re-asserts it under 900px), so this anchors to the strip. */
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe{
    position:absolute;
    top:8px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);
    z-index:4;
    display:none;
    align-items:center;justify-content:center;gap:10px;
    margin:0;
    padding:9px 18px;
    width:max-content;max-width:calc(100% - 24px);
    border-radius:999px;
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.82);
    border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.55);
    box-shadow:0 6px 18px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
    color:#EEE1FB;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:13.5px;font-weight:800;
    letter-spacing:.10em;text-transform:uppercase;
    white-space:nowrap;
    pointer-events:none;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe span,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe span{display:inline-block;font-size:21px;line-height:.7;color:#F6ECFF;animation:pgwNudge 1.5s ease-in-out infinite}
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe .pgw-l,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe .pgw-l{animation-direction:reverse}
  /* the counter repaints on every scroll frame, so it is fixed-width: tabular
     figures plus a min-width sized for the widest reading stop the centred pill
     shimmying as the digits change under the finger. */
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe b.pgw-n,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe b.pgw-n{
    display:inline-block;
    min-width:4.8ch;text-align:center;
    font-weight:800;color:#FFFFFF;letter-spacing:.04em;
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  }
  @keyframes pgwNudge{
    0%,100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:.55}
    50%    {transform:translateX(4px);opacity:1}
  }
  /* the PHOTO strip's pill is phones only -- desktop keeps pg-landing's real
     arrows there. The VIDEO stage has no arrows left at any width, so its pill
     shows at every width. */
  @media (max-width:900px){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe.pgw-on{display:flex}
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe.pgw-on{display:flex}
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-swipe span,
    #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-swipe span{animation:none}
  }

  /* the design name pill -- same look on the video and on the photos below it.
     pointer-events:none so it is a label, not a control. */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-name,
  #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-shot{
    position:absolute;
    bottom:10px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);
    z-index:4;
    max-width:calc(100% - 24px);
    padding:6px 14px;
    border-radius:999px;
    background:rgba(11,7,16,.82);
    border:1px solid rgba(190,140,235,.55);
    box-shadow:0 6px 18px -6px rgba(0,0,0,.8);
    color:#F1EDF7;
    font-family:'Poppins','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
    font-size:12.5px;font-weight:800;
    letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;
    white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;
    text-align:center;
    pointer-events:none;
  }
  @media (max-width:430px){
    #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-name,
    #pgx .pgx-thumbwrap .pgw-shot{font-size:11.5px;padding:5px 12px}
  }

  /* the dots move up to clear the name pill rather than sit behind it */
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dots{
    position:absolute;left:0;right:0;bottom:46px;z-index:5;
    display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap;padding:0 12px;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot{
    width:7px !important;height:7px !important;min-width:0 !important;min-height:0 !important;
    padding:0 !important;margin:0 !important;border:0 !important;border-radius:50% !important;
    background:rgba(255,255,255,.42) !important;background-image:none !important;
    box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.6) !important;cursor:pointer !important;
    animation:none !important;transition:background .15s ease,width .15s ease !important;
  }
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot::before,
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot::after{display:none !important;content:none !important}
  #pgx .pgx-vid .pgw-dot.on{background:#fff !important;width:18px !important;border-radius:999px !important}

  /* NO mobile aspect override. pg-landing squared this slot off at 1:1, which
     is just another box too wide for the footage and showed the same bars. The
     one 990x1080 box and its zoom apply at every width. */
</style>
<script>
(function(){
  /* The hero is display:none for good, so stop it pulling down a full-size copy
     of a photo nobody sees. pg-landing holds its own reference to the element
     and may set a src back on it when a photo is clicked; that costs nothing
     while it is hidden. */
  var hero = document.getElementById('pgx-hero-img');
  if (hero) hero.removeAttribute('src');

  /* Character -> the variant that sells it. Tested in order, so the EX rule
     must stay ABOVE the plain Blastoise one: "Blastoise EX" belongs to the EX
     trio, not to Blastoise Evolution. The last rule is the catch-all for the
     original trio's photos, whose alts name Charizard without an EX. */
  var DESIGNS = [
    /* an alt that names the variant outright wins over any character in it --
       the trio's own alt reads "Charizard Squirtle and Bulbasaur", which would
       otherwise be caught by the Squirtle rule below and labelled Blastoise
       Evolution */
    {re: /legendary poke trio/i,                     v: "Legendary Poke Trio"},
    {re: /blue-?eyes|dark magician|red-?eyes/i,      v: "Blue Eyes, Dark Magician, Red Eyes"},
    {re: /gengar|gastly|haunter/i,                   v: "Gengar Evolution"},
    {re: /(charizard|venusaur|blastoise)\s*ex\b/i,   v: "EX Legendary Poke Trio"},
    {re: /squirtle|wartortle|blastoise/i,            v: "Blastoise Evolution"},
    {re: /eevee|vaporeon|flareon/i,                  v: "Eeveelutions"},
    {re: /mewtwo/i,                                  v: "Mewtwo EX, V, V Star"},
    {re: /one piece/i,                               v: "One Piece"},
    {re: /dragon ball/i,                             v: "Dragon Ball"},
    {re: /goku/i,                                    v: "Goku"},
    {re: /heroic 3d wall art|charizard/i,            v: "Legendary Poke Trio"}
  ];
  function designOf(alt){
    if (!alt) return '';
    for (var i = 0; i < DESIGNS.length; i++){ if (DESIGNS[i].re.test(alt)) return DESIGNS[i].v; }
    return '';
  }

  /* filename -> alt, from the JSON the page shipped with */
  var MEDIA = null;
  function media(){
    if (MEDIA) return MEDIA;
    var tag = document.getElementById('pgw-media');
    if (!tag) return null;
    var arr;
    try { arr = JSON.parse(tag.textContent); } catch (e) { return null; }
    if (!arr || !arr.length) return null;
    MEDIA = {};
    for (var i = 0; i < arr.length; i++){ if (arr[i] && arr[i].s) MEDIA[arr[i].s] = arr[i].a || ''; }
    return MEDIA;
  }

  /* the strip requests a RESIZED copy, so the stored name is looked up by the
     filename stem with Shopify's _700x style suffix stripped -- still a filename
     match, never an index guess */
  function designForImg(img){
    var M = media();
    if (!M || !img) return '';
    var file = ((img.getAttribute('src') || '').split('/').pop() || '').split('?')[0];
    if (M[file] != null) return designOf(M[file]);
    var stem = file.replace(/\.(png|jpg|jpeg|webp|gif)$/i, '').replace(/_\d+x\d*$/, '');
    if (!stem) return '';
    for (var k in M){
      var ks = k.replace(/\.[^.]+$/, '');
      if (ks === stem || ks.indexOf(stem) === 0 || stem.indexOf(ks) === 0) return designOf(M[k]);
    }
    return '';
  }

  /* One clip per variant, in the product's own option order so the dots read
     left-to-right the same way the dropdown does. `v` must stay character-exact
     with the variant option value -- that string IS the key the buy-box selects
     and the cart's variant picker both report, and matching is what points the
     slideshow at the right clip. Renaming a variant in Shopify without editing
     this list leaves that one design falling back to slide 0. `z` is how far
     that clip has to be zoomed to push its baked-in pillarbox off the edges of
     the box; see the ASPECT note above before changing one.

     `r` is playback speed, per clip rather than one global rate: the nine newer
     ones run at 2.5x and the original at its own pace. It is applied in three
     places on purpose -- defaultPlaybackRate before the source is attached,
     because load() resets an element to its defaults and playbackRate is one of
     the things it clears; playbackRate at the swap, for browsers that do not
     carry the default onto a newly attached source; and once more from the play
     watchdog, the only code that runs after a retried play(). Setting it in any
     one of those alone is not enough. */
  var VIDS = [
    {z: 1, r: 1, v: "Legendary Poke Trio", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/372c232efb264da688df7ddecba254c7/372c232efb264da688df7ddecba254c7.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92098881.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Blue Eyes, Dark Magician, Red Eyes", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/9d6a76bbc7504f589d013b52660475b2/9d6a76bbc7504f589d013b52660475b2.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92296781.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Gengar Evolution", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/34da042ae6e64494be9fdfcbfc58b43d/34da042ae6e64494be9fdfcbfc58b43d.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296780.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Blastoise Evolution", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/d5d5246b3616411ca0ed05f1b982f44c/d5d5246b3616411ca0ed05f1b982f44c.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296779.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "EX Legendary Poke Trio", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/8a2173f6b8ca40c2b5819534a0be89f9/8a2173f6b8ca40c2b5819534a0be89f9.HD-720p-4.5Mbps-92296778.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Eeveelutions", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/74b84084bd414500a949f60c972172b0/74b84084bd414500a949f60c972172b0.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296777.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Mewtwo EX, V, V Star", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/28bedf6bfa444dc68d3aa93170d0f0d8/28bedf6bfa444dc68d3aa93170d0f0d8.HD-720p-2.1Mbps-92296776.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "One Piece", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/79e22ac3490d404f80990b5e764ec531/79e22ac3490d404f80990b5e764ec531.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296775.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Dragon Ball", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/8c2e1751c07744dd9846e44ad1050462/8c2e1751c07744dd9846e44ad1050462.HD-720p-3.0Mbps-92296774.mp4"},
    {z: 1.66, r: 2.5, v: "Goku", u: "https://cdn.shopify.com/videos/c/vp/1a5d4284598e442da8c01b753ee09909/1a5d4284598e442da8c01b753ee09909.HD-720p-2.1Mbps-92296773.mp4"}
  ];

  function boot(){
    var box = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-vid');
    if (!box || box.querySelector('.pgw-stage')) return false;

    var stage = document.createElement('div');
    stage.className = 'pgw-stage';

    function mk(){
      var v = document.createElement('video');
      v.className = 'pgw-v';
      v.muted = true; v.loop = true; v.playsInline = true;
      v.setAttribute('muted','');           /* iOS autoplays only with the attribute present */
      v.setAttribute('loop','');
      v.setAttribute('playsinline','');
      v.setAttribute('preload','none');     /* nothing is fetched until a slide is asked for */
      stage.appendChild(v);
      return v;
    }
    var a = mk(), b = mk(), front = a, cur = -1, want = -1, token = 0, inflight = null;

    /* the pill that replaced the two chevrons -- same markup as the photo
       strip's, so the two read as one idea. aria-hidden and pointer-events:none:
       it is a label on the gesture, not a control, and the dots below remain the
       keyboard- and screen-reader-reachable way through the slideshow. */
    var swipe = document.createElement('div');
    swipe.className = 'pgw-swipe pgw-on';
    swipe.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    swipe.innerHTML = '<span class="pgw-l">&lsaquo;</span> Swipe &middot; <b class="pgw-n"></b> <span>&rsaquo;</span>';
    var swipeN = swipe.querySelector('.pgw-n');

    var dots = document.createElement('div');
    dots.className = 'pgw-dots';
    var dotEls = VIDS.map(function(item, i){
      var d = document.createElement('button');
      d.type = 'button'; d.className = 'pgw-dot';
      d.setAttribute('aria-label', item.v);
      d.addEventListener('click', function(){ show(i); });
      dots.appendChild(d);
      return d;
    });

    var nameEl = document.createElement('div');
    nameEl.className = 'pgw-name';
    nameEl.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');

    stage.appendChild(swipe); stage.appendChild(dots); stage.appendChild(nameEl);

    /* pg-landing's own <video> is replaced only now that the stage is built, so
       a failure above leaves the original single video untouched on screen. */
    box.innerHTML = '';
    box.appendChild(stage);

    function paintDots(){
      dotEls.forEach(function(d, i){ d.classList.toggle('on', i === want); });
      /* paintDots() is the one funnel every slide change goes through -- arrow-
         less swipe, dot, or a variant chosen elsewhere on the page -- so the
         counter cannot drift out of step with what is on screen. Written only on
         a change: this element sits inside #pgx, which carries a childList
         MutationObserver further down, and an unconditional write would wake it
         on every swap for nothing. */
      if (swipeN){
        var pos = (want + 1) + '/' + VIDS.length;
        if (swipeN.textContent !== pos) swipeN.textContent = pos;
      }
    }

    /* THE SUPERVISOR -- a frozen slide fixes itself within a second.

       This replaced a burst of retries fired once per swap, which looked right
       and protected almost nothing: play() flips `paused` to false the moment it
       is called, long before a frame is on screen, so the retry loop saw
       "playing" on its first look and stopped. Anything that stopped playback
       AFTER that -- a rejected play promise, a stall while the opening clip was
       still hogging the connection, a decode hiccup, the tab being backgrounded
       -- left a still frame with nothing watching it. That matches the report
       exactly: the clip sat frozen, and paging away and back (a fresh load, by
       then cached) played it.

       So playback is judged on whether currentTime actually MOVES, not on the
       paused flag, and it is judged forever rather than for one second after a
       swap. Every clip loops, so a running slide always advances; a slide that
       has not advanced between two ticks is wrong no matter how it got there,
       and gets another play(). Calling play() on a video that is already playing
       is a no-op, so the common path costs nothing. */
    var lastT = -1, lastFront = null;
    setInterval(function(){
      var v = front;
      if (!v || !v.getAttribute('src')) return;
      if (v !== lastFront){ lastFront = v; lastT = -1; }   /* new slide: no history yet */

      var rate = (cur >= 0 && VIDS[cur]) ? VIDS[cur].r : 1;
      if (v.playbackRate !== rate) v.playbackRate = rate;

      var t = v.currentTime;
      var moved = (lastT >= 0) && (t > lastT + 0.01 || t < lastT);  /* t < lastT = looped */
      var stuck = v.paused || (lastT >= 0 && !moved && v.readyState >= 2);
      lastT = t;
      if (stuck){
        var p = v.play();
        if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){});
      }
    }, 700);

    /* Last resort: if the browser refused to autoplay this element outright, the
       first tap or key anywhere on the page is a user gesture and lifts that
       block. Costs nothing when playback is already fine. */
    function nudge(){
      var v = front;
      if (v && v.paused){ var p = v.play(); if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){}); }
    }
    ['pointerdown','touchstart','keydown'].forEach(function(ev){
      document.addEventListener(ev, nudge, true);
    });

    function show(i){
      if (i < 0 || i >= VIDS.length || i === want) return;
      want = i;                            /* the slide ASKED for, updated now */
      paintDots();

      /* WHICH ELEMENT THE CLIP LOADS INTO. Normally the one that is not on
         screen. But when a slide is ALREADY loading, that pending element is the
         off-screen one -- so this must reuse it rather than work out a fresh
         answer from `front`, which still points at the visible clip and would
         hand back the element the shopper is watching.

         That is the bug this replaced. Two show() calls overlapping (the init
         call still loading when a variant change or an arrow arrives) had the
         second one take `front` as its incoming element: it reset the src of the
         clip on screen, then added and immediately removed its own `on` class,
         leaving the visible slide invisible AND paused by the outgoing timer.
         Recovery needed a page past and back, which is exactly what was
         reported for the two slides nearest the start -- the ones reachable
         while the opening clip is still loading. */
      var incoming = inflight || ((front === a) ? b : a);
      if (incoming === front) return;
      inflight = incoming;

      var mine = ++token;                  /* a later request wins; this one goes quiet */
      var swapped = false;

      function swap(){
        if (swapped || mine !== token) return;
        swapped = true;
        inflight = null;

        /* read the outgoing element HERE, not when show() was called: a swap
           that happened in between would have moved it. */
        var outgoing = front;

        incoming.style.transform = 'scale(' + VIDS[i].z + ')';
        incoming.playbackRate = VIDS[i].r;
        incoming.classList.add('on');
        var p = incoming.play();
        if (p && p.catch) p.catch(function(){});

        if (outgoing !== incoming){
          outgoing.classList.remove('on');
          /* `outgoing !== front` and not a token check: the token moves on every
             request, which used to leave a superseded clip playing forever, and
             a stale timer could pause whatever had since become visible. Asking
             whether this element is still off screen is the question that
             actually matters. */
          setTimeout(function(){
            if (outgoing !== front) { try { outgoing.pause(); } catch(e){} }
          }, 320);
        }

        front = incoming; cur = i; paintDots();
        nameEl.textContent = VIDS[i].v;
      }

      /* drop the previous attempt's listener -- once:true only removes it if it
         actually fired, and a load that was superseded never fires */
      if (incoming.pgwOn) incoming.removeEventListener('loadeddata', incoming.pgwOn);
      incoming.pgwOn = swap;
      incoming.addEventListener('loadeddata', swap, {once: true});
      incoming.defaultPlaybackRate = VIDS[i].r;   /* what load() will restore to */
      incoming.src = VIDS[i].u;
      try { incoming.load(); } catch(e){}
      /* loadeddata does not fire on every browser and network combination -- swap
         anyway rather than leaving the shopper on the previous design's clip. */
      setTimeout(swap, 1800);
    }

    /* Stepped from `want`, not `cur`. `cur` only moves once a clip has loaded, so
       on a slow connection every step during the wait recomputed the SAME
       neighbour and restarted the same download -- measured in a throttled
       harness, six inputs advanced the slideshow by one slide and kept
       cancelling the load that would have ended the wait. Stepping from what was
       last requested makes each one count. */
    function step(dir){
      show(((want + dir) % VIDS.length + VIDS.length) % VIDS.length);
    }

    /* THE SWIPE. See note 6 at the top: the stage is not a scroller, so this is
       what actually moves the slideshow now that the arrows are gone.

       Pointer events cover touch, pen and a desktop mouse drag in one path. The
       gesture stays undecided until it has moved 10px further sideways than
       down; only then does it claim the drag and start calling preventDefault,
       so a finger that lands on the video and pulls DOWN still scrolls the page
       normally. AXIS is latched once decided -- re-testing every move would let
       a curved drag flip mid-gesture.

       setPointerCapture keeps the moves coming when the finger leaves the video,
       which is common on a short stage, and guarantees the matching up/cancel.
       A gesture starting on a dot is left alone so the dots stay clickable. */
    var sx = 0, sy = 0, sid = null, axis = 0;   /* axis: 0 undecided, 1 horizontal, -1 vertical */
    var THRESH = 40;                            /* px of travel that counts as a swipe */

    stage.addEventListener('pointerdown', function(e){
      if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' && e.button !== 0) return;
      if (e.target && e.target.closest && e.target.closest('.pgw-dot')) return;
      sid = e.pointerId; sx = e.clientX; sy = e.clientY; axis = 0;
      try { stage.setPointerCapture(sid); } catch (err){}
    });

    stage.addEventListener('pointermove', function(e){
      if (sid === null || e.pointerId !== sid) return;
      var dx = e.clientX - sx, dy = e.clientY - sy;
      if (axis === 0){
        if (Math.abs(dx) > 10 && Math.abs(dx) > Math.abs(dy)){
          axis = 1;
          stage.classList.add('pgw-drag');
        } else if (Math.abs(dy) > 10){
          axis = -1;                            /* the page's gesture, not ours */
        }
      }
      if (axis === 1 && e.cancelable) e.preventDefault();
    }, {passive: false});

    function endDrag(e){
      if (sid === null || (e && e.pointerId !== undefined && e.pointerId !== sid)) return;
      var dx = (e && e.clientX !== undefined) ? e.clientX - sx : 0;
      var was = axis;
      sid = null; axis = 0;
      stage.classList.remove('pgw-drag');
      if (was !== 1 || Math.abs(dx) < THRESH) return;
      step(dx < 0 ? 1 : -1);                    /* drag left = next, as the content follows the finger */
    }
    stage.addEventListener('pointerup', endDrag);
    stage.addEventListener('pointercancel', endDrag);

    /* the slideshow keeps a keyboard route now that the buttons are gone */
    stage.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
    stage.setAttribute('role', 'group');
    stage.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Product video slideshow');
    stage.addEventListener('keydown', function(e){
      if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft'){ step(-1); e.preventDefault(); }
      else if (e.key === 'ArrowRight'){ step(1); e.preventDefault(); }
    });

    function indexOf(s){
      if (!s) return -1;
      s = String(s).trim();
      for (var i = 0; i < VIDS.length; i++){ if (VIDS[i].v === s) return i; }
      return -1;
    }

    /* ANY select that reports a known design name moves the slideshow. Delegated
       from the document on purpose: this has to cover the buy-box dropdowns
       (pg-landing's [data-pgx-size] selects, which exist at load) AND the
       variant picker inside the cart drawer, which is rebuilt from scratch every
       time the cart changes -- a listener bound directly to that element would
       be thrown away with it. Capture phase, so it still fires if a handler
       downstream stops the event. A select on the page whose value happens to
       equal a variant name would also drive it; nothing else on this template
       carries those strings. */
    document.addEventListener('change', function(e){
      var t = e.target;
      if (!t || t.tagName !== 'SELECT') return;
      var i = indexOf(t.value);
      if (i < 0 && t.options && t.selectedIndex >= 0){
        var o = t.options[t.selectedIndex];
        if (o) i = indexOf(o.textContent);
      }
      if (i >= 0) show(i);
    }, true);

    /* open on whatever the buy box is already showing */
    var s1 = document.querySelector('#pgx [data-pgx-size="s1"]');
    var start = s1 ? indexOf(s1.value) : -1;
    show(start >= 0 ? start : 0);
    return true;
  }

  /* ---- the same name pill under the photo strip ----
     Driven by whichever photo is centred in the strip. Cheap enough to run on
     an animation frame: the button centres are measured once and cached, and
     re-measured only when the strip node, its length or its width changes, so a
     frame costs a handful of subtractions and no layout read. */
  var MIDS = null;
  function stripLabel(){
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    if (!strip) return;
    var btns = strip.querySelectorAll('button');
    if (!btns.length) return;

    if (!MIDS || MIDS.strip !== strip || MIDS.n !== btns.length || MIDS.w !== strip.clientWidth){
      var v = [];
      for (var i = 0; i < btns.length; i++) v.push(btns[i].offsetLeft + btns[i].offsetWidth / 2);
      MIDS = {strip: strip, n: btns.length, w: strip.clientWidth, v: v};
    }

    /* the photo whose centre is nearest the strip's */
    var mids = MIDS.v, mid = strip.scrollLeft + strip.clientWidth / 2, best = -1, bestD = Infinity;
    for (var j = 0; j < mids.length; j++){
      var d = Math.abs(mids[j] - mid);
      if (d < bestD){ bestD = d; best = j; }
    }
    if (best < 0) return;

    /* the swipe pill's counter */
    var cnt = wrap.querySelector('.pgw-swipe .pgw-n');
    if (cnt){
      var pos = (best + 1) + '/' + btns.length;
      if (cnt.textContent !== pos) cnt.textContent = pos;
    }

    var text = designForImg(btns[best].querySelector('img'));
    var tag = wrap.querySelector('.pgw-shot');
    if (!text){ if (tag && tag.parentNode) tag.parentNode.removeChild(tag); return; }
    if (!tag){
      tag = document.createElement('div');
      tag.className = 'pgw-shot';
      tag.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      wrap.appendChild(tag);
    }
    if (tag.textContent !== text) tag.textContent = text;
  }

  /* the swipe hint. Inserted only once the strip actually has somewhere to
     scroll to, and re-inserted whenever pg-landing rebuilds the gallery and
     throws it away. Never removed on scroll -- see the note at the top. */
  function hint(){
    var wrap = document.querySelector('#pgx .pgx-thumbwrap');
    if (!wrap) return;
    if (wrap.querySelector('.pgw-swipe')) return;      /* already there */
    var strip = wrap.querySelector('.pgx-thumbs');
    if (!strip) return;
    if (strip.scrollWidth <= strip.clientWidth + 4) return;   /* nothing to swipe to */

    var el = document.createElement('div');
    el.className = 'pgw-swipe pgw-on';
    el.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    el.innerHTML = '<span class="pgw-l">&lsaquo;</span> Swipe &middot; <b class="pgw-n"></b> <span>&rsaquo;</span>';
    wrap.appendChild(el);
    stripLabel();                                       /* fill the counter now */
  }

  /* `scroll` does not bubble, but it DOES capture, so listening on the document
     catches the strip's own scrolling and survives pg-landing throwing the
     gallery away and rebuilding it -- a listener bound to the strip node would
     go with it. Repainted per animation frame rather than on a debounce: a
     debounce is cleared by every new scroll event, so through a continuous swipe
     the label would sit on the previous design until the finger lifted. */
  var sraf = 0;
  document.addEventListener('scroll', function(e){
    var t = e.target;
    if (!t || !t.classList || !t.classList.contains('pgx-thumbs')) return;
    if (!sraf) sraf = requestAnimationFrame(function(){ sraf = 0; stripLabel(); });
  }, {capture: true, passive: true});

  window.addEventListener('resize', function(){ MIDS = null; hint(); stripLabel(); }, {passive: true});

  /* pg-landing rebuilds the gallery after its own fetch, which throws the pill
     away and moves every button, so the cache is dropped and the label redrawn.
     Appending the pill is itself a childList mutation, so this would loop if it
     wrote on every pass -- it settles because a second pass with the same text
     writes nothing. */
  var pgxRoot = document.getElementById('pgx');
  if (pgxRoot && window.MutationObserver){
    var queuedLbl = false;
    new MutationObserver(function(){
      if (queuedLbl) return;
      queuedLbl = true;
      MIDS = null;
      setTimeout(function(){ queuedLbl = false; hint(); stripLabel(); }, 80);
    }).observe(pgxRoot, {childList: true, subtree: true});
  }
  hint(); stripLabel();
  [300, 900, 2000, 4000].forEach(function(ms){ setTimeout(function(){ hint(); stripLabel(); }, ms); });

  /* pg-landing renders .pgx-vid server-side, so it is normally there already;
     the retry only covers this section being evaluated before it. */
  if (!boot()){
    var tries = 0;
    var t = setInterval(function(){
      if (boot() || ++tries > 40) clearInterval(t);
    }, 150);
  }
})();
</script>

